{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-7-014-spring-2005", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["7.014"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Introductory Biology", "course_slug": "7-014-introductory-biology-spring-2005", "term": "Spring", "year": 2005, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2005", "language": "en", "departments": ["Biology"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Graham Walker", "Dr. Julia Khodor", "Dr. Michelle Mischke", "Prof. Penny Chisholm"], "subject_primary": "Biology", "subject_secondary": [], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Biology", "specialties": []}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Exam Solutions", "Exams", "Lecture Notes", "Lecture Videos", "Problem Set Solutions", "Problem Sets"], "description": "The MIT Biology Department core courses, 7.012 , 7.013 , and 7.014, all cover the same core material, which includes the fundamental principles of biochemistry, genetics, molecular biology, and cell biology. Biological function at the molecular level is particularly emphasized and covers the structure and regulation of genes, as well as, the structure and synthesis of proteins, how these molecules are integrated into cells, and how these cells are integrated into multicellular systems and organisms. In addition, each version of the subject has its own distinctive material. 7.014 focuses on the application of these fundamental principles, toward an understanding of microorganisms as geochemical agents responsible for the evolution and renewal of the biosphere and of their role in human health and disease. Acknowledgements The study materials, problem sets, and quiz materials used during Spring 2005 for 7.014 include contributions from past instructors, teaching assistants, and other members of the MIT Biology Department affiliated with course 7.014. Sinc